#!/usr/bin/bash
# shellcheck shell=bash
# ==============================================================================
# AquariusOS — "is there a newer copy of this app in the user's home folder?"
# ==============================================================================
# THIS FILE IS NOT A PROGRAM YOU RUN. It is a set of shell functions that other
# scripts read in ("source") so they can all agree on the answer. The shebang
# line at the top is only there so that editors and shellcheck know it is bash.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS EXISTS, IN PLAIN ENGLISH
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AquariusOS is an immutable operating system. The whole of /usr — which is where
# Aquarius Editor and Aquarius Writer live — is READ-ONLY while the machine is
# running. That is a
# feature: it is why the OS cannot be broken by a bad install and why you can
# always roll back to yesterday's version.
#
# It has one awkward consequence. An app baked into /usr CANNOT UPDATE ITSELF.
# Normally that is fine — a new version of the app arrives with the next OS
# update. But an OS update is a whole new image, it is downloaded in the
# background, and it only takes effect after a reboot. If we shipped a bad bug in
# one of our apps on a Friday, everybody would live with it until they rebooted.
#
# So each of our apps is also allowed to download a newer copy of ITSELF into the
# user's own home folder, where writing is allowed. This file is the OS half of
# that arrangement: at launch it looks for that downloaded copy, works out
# whether it is genuinely newer than the one baked into the OS, and says which
# one should start. It is written to know nothing about any particular app — the
# app's name is handed to it — so both launchers share one copy of these rules.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE AGREEMENT WITH THE APP  (do not change one side of this alone)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything lives under one folder, called the OVERLAY:
#
#     ~/.local/share/aquarius/aquarius-editor/
#     ├── versions/
#     │   ├── 0.4.0/          a complete unpacked copy of the app, AppRun at its
#     │   └── 0.4.1/          top level — exactly the shape /usr/lib/aquarius has
#     └── current -> versions/0.4.1
#
#   * The app's updater writes a new folder under versions/. Its name is the
#     plain version number and nothing else: "0.4.1", never "v0.4.1".
#   * When the download is complete and verified, the app re-points the "current"
#     link at it. Re-pointing a link is instantaneous, so there is no moment when
#     "current" leads to a half-written folder.
#   * The OS tells the app that this arrangement is in force by setting two
#     environment variables before starting it (see aq_overlay_prepare below):
#         AQUARIUS_OS_MANAGED_INSTALL=1
#         AQUARIUS_UPDATE_OVERLAY_DIR=~/.local/share/aquarius/aquarius-editor
#   * The OS says which version IT has by writing a plain text file at
#     /usr/lib/aquarius/aquarius-editor/VERSION containing, for example, "0.3.0".
#     It is written during the image build — see build_files/creator-apps.sh.
#
# ⚠️ The overlay path is written out literally rather than following
#    XDG_DATA_HOME. That is deliberate: the app's updater targets that exact
#    path, and a launcher that looked somewhere else would quietly stop finding
#    updates for anybody who sets that variable. If the path ever changes it must
#    change in both places, on the same day.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RULES THIS FILE FOLLOWS
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   1. The downloaded copy is used ONLY if its version is genuinely NEWER than
#      the built-in one, compared as version numbers and not as text. (As text,
#      "0.9.0" sorts after "0.10.0", which would pin everybody to an old build
#      forever. This is a real and common bug; there is a proper comparison
#      below and a test suite for it.)
#   2. A BROKEN DOWNLOAD MUST NEVER STOP THE APP FROM STARTING. Every single
#      thing that can go wrong out here — a link pointing at nothing, a folder
#      with a nonsense name, a half-deleted folder, no home directory at all —
#      ends the same way: write a line in the log, start the copy that came with
#      the OS, and get out of the way.
#   3. When the OS catches up (its built-in version is the same or newer), the
#      downloaded copies are deleted. They are roughly 2 GB each and they are now
#      just a stale cache; the app makes a new one the next time it updates.
#      Nothing is EVER deleted while the downloaded copy is the newer one.
#
# Read alongside: /usr/libexec/aquarius-app-launch (what actually starts the app)
#                 /usr/bin/aquarius-editor          (the app-specific front doors)
#                 /usr/bin/aquarius-writer
# ==============================================================================

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT A VERSION NUMBER LOOKS LIKE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Three numbers separated by dots — 0.3.0, 1.12.4 — optionally followed by a
# "-something" pre-release part (0.4.0-beta.1) and/or a "+something" build part.
# That is the "semantic versioning" convention the Aquarius releases follow.
#
# Each number is capped at nine digits. Not fussiness: the comparison below does
# real arithmetic on these, and a 30-digit number read out of a stray folder name
# would overflow the shell's arithmetic and produce an error instead of an
# answer. Nine digits is far more version numbers than anyone will ever ship.
AQ_SEMVER_RE='^([0-9]{1,9})\.([0-9]{1,9})\.([0-9]{1,9})(-([0-9A-Za-z.-]+))?(\+[0-9A-Za-z.-]+)?$'

# aq_semver_valid <text>
#   Succeeds (exit code 0) when <text> is a version number of the shape above.
#   Note there is no leading "v": folder names are bare numbers, so "v0.4.1" is
#   deliberately NOT valid here.
aq_semver_valid() {
    [[ "$1" =~ $AQ_SEMVER_RE ]]
}

# aq_semver_compare <a> <b>
#   Prints  1  if a is newer than b
#           0  if they are the same version
#          -1  if a is older than b
#   and exits 2 without printing anything if either one is not a version number.
#
# HOW IT COMPARES, AND WHY IT IS NOT JUST "IS THIS TEXT BIGGER"
# It takes the three numbers apart and compares them as NUMBERS, largest part
# first — so 0.10.0 correctly beats 0.9.0, which plain text comparison gets
# wrong. The "10#" in front of each number tells the shell "read this in ordinary
# base ten"; without it a part written as 08 would be read as a broken octal
# number and the comparison would fail outright.
#
# PRE-RELEASES (0.4.0-beta.1) are treated the way the semver convention says: a
# pre-release is OLDER than the finished version with the same three numbers, so
# 0.4.0-beta.1 < 0.4.0, while still being newer than 0.3.9. Two different
# pre-releases of the SAME version are settled by plain text order, which is an
# approximation of the full rule and is more than enough here — we never ship two
# pre-releases of one version to the same machine.
aq_semver_compare() {
    local a="$1" b="$2"
    local a_maj a_min a_pat a_pre
    local b_maj b_min b_pat b_pre
    local pair x y

    [[ "$a" =~ $AQ_SEMVER_RE ]] || return 2
    a_maj="${BASH_REMATCH[1]}"; a_min="${BASH_REMATCH[2]}"
    a_pat="${BASH_REMATCH[3]}"; a_pre="${BASH_REMATCH[5]:-}"

    [[ "$b" =~ $AQ_SEMVER_RE ]] || return 2
    b_maj="${BASH_REMATCH[1]}"; b_min="${BASH_REMATCH[2]}"
    b_pat="${BASH_REMATCH[3]}"; b_pre="${BASH_REMATCH[5]:-}"

    for pair in "${a_maj}:${b_maj}" "${a_min}:${b_min}" "${a_pat}:${b_pat}"; do
        x="${pair%%:*}"
        y="${pair##*:}"
        if [ "$((10#$x))" -gt "$((10#$y))" ]; then printf '%s\n' 1;  return 0; fi
        if [ "$((10#$x))" -lt "$((10#$y))" ]; then printf '%s\n' -1; return 0; fi
    done

    # The three numbers are equal. A finished version beats a pre-release of it.
    if [ -z "$a_pre" ] && [ -n "$b_pre" ]; then printf '%s\n' 1;  return 0; fi
    if [ -n "$a_pre" ] && [ -z "$b_pre" ]; then printf '%s\n' -1; return 0; fi
    if [ "$a_pre" = "$b_pre" ]; then printf '%s\n' 0; return 0; fi

    # Two different pre-releases of the same version: plain text order.
    if [[ "$a_pre" > "$b_pre" ]]; then printf '%s\n' 1; else printf '%s\n' -1; fi
    return 0
}

# aq_semver_newer <a> <b>
#   Succeeds only when a is a version number, b is a version number, and a is
#   strictly newer than b. Anything else — including either one being nonsense —
#   fails, which is the safe answer for every caller here.
aq_semver_newer() {
    local result
    result="$(aq_semver_compare "$1" "$2")" || return 1
    [ "$result" = "1" ]
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Collecting the story for the log
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything decided here is written into the app's log file, so that a bench
# debugging session can see which copy started and why in the first few lines.
# The lines are gathered up in AQ_LAUNCH_NOTES and handed to the launcher, which
# is the thing that owns the log file.
aq__note() {
    if [ -n "${AQ_LAUNCH_NOTES:-}" ]; then
        AQ_LAUNCH_NOTES="${AQ_LAUNCH_NOTES}
$1"
    else
        AQ_LAUNCH_NOTES="$1"
    fi
}

# aq__read_version <file>
#   Prints the version number in <file>, or nothing at all if the file is
#   missing, unreadable, or does not contain a valid version number.
#
#   Only the first 256 bytes are read. A VERSION file is nine characters long;
#   reading a bounded amount means that pointing this at the wrong file by
#   accident can never make the launcher sit there swallowing a two-gigabyte
#   video before it decides the contents are not a version number.
aq__read_version() {
    local file="$1" value=""
    [ -r "$file" ] || return 0
    value="$(head -c 256 -- "$file" 2>/dev/null | head -n 1 | tr -d ' \t\r\n')"
    aq_semver_valid "$value" || return 0
    printf '%s\n' "$value"
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Throwing away update caches the OS has caught up with
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Each downloaded copy is roughly two gigabytes. Once the OS itself ships that
# version or a newer one, the downloaded copy is dead weight — the app would
# never start it again, and the app knows how to download a fresh one whenever it
# next needs to.
#
# THE ONE RULE: nothing is deleted unless its version is the same as, or older
# than, the version built into the OS. This function is only ever called when the
# built-in copy is the one being started, so a newer download is never in reach
# of it. Read that twice before changing anything here.
#
# It also removes a "current" link that now leads nowhere, which is litter left
# behind after its folder goes.
aq__overlay_cleanup() {   # aq__overlay_cleanup <overlay-root> <built-in-version>
    local root="$1" baked_version="$2"
    local versions dir name comparison

    # Refuse to touch anything that is not inside this user's own home folder.
    # A deletion loop is exactly the wrong place to assume the path is sensible.
    [ -n "${HOME:-}" ] || return 0
    case "$root" in
        "${HOME}"/*) : ;;
        *) return 0 ;;
    esac

    versions="${root}/versions"
    [ -d "$versions" ] || return 0

    for dir in "$versions"/*; do
        # When versions/ is empty the loop still runs once, with the pattern
        # itself as the value. This is the check that spots that.
        [ -e "$dir" ] || continue
        # Never remove, and never follow, a symlink here. Only real folders that
        # we can name a version for are ours to delete.
        [ -L "$dir" ] && continue
        [ -d "$dir" ] || continue

        name="$(basename -- "$dir")"
        aq_semver_valid "$name" || continue
        comparison="$(aq_semver_compare "$name" "$baked_version")" || continue
        [ "$comparison" = "1" ] && continue   # newer than the OS — not ours to touch

        if rm -rf -- "$dir" 2>/dev/null; then
            aq__note "update cache: deleted ${dir} — version ${name} is no newer than the ${baked_version} built into AquariusOS, so it was only taking up space."
        else
            aq__note "update cache: tried to delete ${dir} and could not. Left alone; this is harmless."
        fi
    done

    # A link pointing at a folder that is no longer there.
    if [ -L "${root}/current" ] && [ ! -e "${root}/current" ]; then
        if rm -f -- "${root}/current" 2>/dev/null; then
            aq__note "update cache: removed the leftover 'current' link, which pointed at nothing."
        fi
    fi
}

# ==============================================================================
# THE ONE FUNCTION CALLERS ACTUALLY USE
# ==============================================================================
# aq_overlay_prepare <app-id> [folder-the-built-in-apps-live-in]
#
# The second argument exists only so that the test suite can point the whole
# thing at a made-up folder full of made-up apps. Real callers pass one argument
# and get /usr/lib/aquarius, which is where the apps really are. It is a function
# argument rather than an environment variable on purpose: an environment
# variable could be set by anything at all and would be a way to make the
# launcher start a different program.
#
# It sets two variables for the caller to read:
#
#   AQ_INSTALL_ROOT   the folder the app should be started from. Either the
#                     built-in /usr/lib/aquarius/<app-id> or a newer downloaded
#                     copy in the user's home folder.
#   AQ_LAUNCH_NOTES   a few lines of plain English explaining that choice, for
#                     the launcher to write into the app's log file.
#
# and exports the two variables the app itself reads (see the agreement above).
#
# It never fails. Every problem it meets becomes a note and the built-in copy.
#
# BOTH OF OUR APPS USE THIS. /usr/bin/aquarius-editor and /usr/bin/aquarius-writer
# each call it with their own name, and nothing in this file knows or cares which
# is which. Adding a third app is the same four lines in its launcher plus a
# VERSION file from build_files/creator-apps.sh (which writes one for every app in
# its table already). The launcher wiring is checked by tests/test-aquarius-overlay.sh.
aq_overlay_prepare() {
    local app_id="$1"
    local baked_root="${2:-/usr/lib/aquarius}"
    local baked_dir="${baked_root}/${app_id}"
    local overlay_root baked_version current target overlay_version comparison why

    AQ_INSTALL_ROOT="$baked_dir"
    AQ_LAUNCH_NOTES=""

    # True whichever copy ends up starting: this app was installed by the
    # operating system, not by the user dropping a file somewhere. The app uses
    # it to decide that it is allowed to manage its own updates this way.
    export AQUARIUS_OS_MANAGED_INSTALL=1

    # No home folder means no writable place for an update to have been put, and
    # no safe place to delete anything from. Nothing to do.
    if [ -z "${HOME:-}" ]; then
        aq__note "update overlay: this session has no home folder, so no downloaded update can exist. Starting the copy built into AquariusOS."
        return 0
    fi

    overlay_root="${HOME}/.local/share/aquarius/${app_id}"
    export AQUARIUS_UPDATE_OVERLAY_DIR="$overlay_root"

    # --- which version does the OS itself have? -------------------------------
    baked_version="$(aq__read_version "${baked_dir}/VERSION")"
    if [ -z "$baked_version" ]; then
        # Without this we cannot say whether anything is newer, and we certainly
        # cannot say what is superseded — so we compare nothing and delete
        # nothing. This should be impossible: the image build writes the file and
        # both the build and CI refuse to ship without it.
        aq__note "update overlay: ${baked_dir}/VERSION is missing or unreadable, so there is no version to compare a downloaded update against. Starting the copy built into AquariusOS, and deleting nothing."
        return 0
    fi

    # --- is there a downloaded copy, and what version is it? ------------------
    current="${overlay_root}/current"
    target=""
    overlay_version=""

    if [ -L "$current" ] || [ -e "$current" ]; then
        # readlink -f follows the link all the way to the real folder, and prints
        # nothing if the trail ends at something that does not exist.
        target="$(readlink -f -- "$current" 2>/dev/null || true)"
        if [ -z "$target" ] || [ ! -d "$target" ]; then
            aq__note "update overlay: '${current}' does not lead to a folder — the download it pointed at is gone. Starting the copy built into AquariusOS."
            target=""
        else
            overlay_version="$(basename -- "$target")"
            if ! aq_semver_valid "$overlay_version"; then
                aq__note "update overlay: '${current}' leads to ${target}, and '${overlay_version}' is not a version number like 0.4.1. Starting the copy built into AquariusOS."
                target=""
                overlay_version=""
            fi
        fi
    fi

    # --- nothing usable downloaded: start the built-in copy, tidy up ----------
    if [ -z "$overlay_version" ]; then
        aq__note "versions: AquariusOS has ${baked_version}; no usable downloaded update in ${overlay_root}."
        aq__overlay_cleanup "$overlay_root" "$baked_version"
        return 0
    fi

    # --- the comparison this whole file is for --------------------------------
    comparison="$(aq_semver_compare "$overlay_version" "$baked_version")" || comparison=""
    if [ "$comparison" != "1" ]; then
        aq__note "versions: AquariusOS has ${baked_version}, the downloaded update is ${overlay_version}. The built-in copy is the same or newer, so it wins."
        aq__overlay_cleanup "$overlay_root" "$baked_version"
        return 0
    fi

    # --- it is newer. Is it actually startable? -------------------------------
    # A cheap look before we commit to it. The launcher checks the whole app far
    # more thoroughly a moment later and will still fall back to the built-in
    # copy if anything is wrong, so this only has to catch the obvious wreckage.
    why=""
    if [ ! -r "$target" ] || [ ! -x "$target" ]; then
        why="its folder cannot be opened"
    elif [ ! -e "${target}/AppRun" ]; then
        why="it has no AppRun file, so there is nothing in it to start"
    elif [ ! -x "${target}/AppRun" ]; then
        why="its AppRun file is not marked as runnable"
    elif [ -e "${target}/${app_id}" ] && [ ! -x "${target}/${app_id}" ]; then
        why="the program inside it (${app_id}) is not marked as runnable"
    fi

    if [ -n "$why" ]; then
        aq__note "update overlay: version ${overlay_version} IS newer than the ${baked_version} built into AquariusOS, but ${why} (${target}). Starting the built-in copy instead. Nothing has been deleted — the newer download is still there to be looked at."
        return 0
    fi

    # shellcheck disable=SC2034  # this is the whole output of the function: the
    #                              caller (/usr/bin/aquarius-editor, or
    #                              /usr/bin/aquarius-writer) reads it.
    AQ_INSTALL_ROOT="$target"
    aq__note "versions: AquariusOS has ${baked_version}, the downloaded update is ${overlay_version}. STARTING THE DOWNLOADED UPDATE from ${target}."
    return 0
}
